        "key": "gsm8k-train-6450:new_record",
        "raw_call_sha256": "9e513b3e090055007baa670f99e5ea068e7193fdca247a4c44da458e947c9433",
        "student_prefix": [
          {
            "content": "Trisha just got a job offer for an hourly job that pays 15 dollars an hour, 40 hours per week. She has been told to estimate that 20 percent of her pay will be withheld to pay taxes, unemployment insurance, and social security. Assuming Trisha works 52 weeks in a year, what is her annual \"take-home\" pay?",
            "role": "user"
          },
          {
            "content": "First, I calculate Trisha's weekly gross pay: 15 dollars/hour * 40 hours/week = 600 dollars/week. Next, I find out how much is withheld for taxes, unemployment insurance, and social security: 20% of 600 dollars = 0.20 * 600 = 120 dollars. This means her weekly take-home pay is 600 dollars - 120 dollars = 480 dollars. To find her annual take-home pay, I multiply her weekly take-home pay by the number of weeks she works in a year: 480 dollars/week * 52 weeks/year = 24,960 dollars/year. \n\nFINAL: 24960",
            "role": "assistant"
          },
          {
            "content": "Write your own reusable record of this solved task.",
            "role": "user"
          }
        ],
        "student_prefix_sha256": "188938231cb5dff7a24b8b2ccb39508a5e38214742dc133e7e372d1b48ee5bde",
        "target": "Given the hourly wage of $15 per hour, the number of hours worked per week (40), the percentage of pay withheld (20%), and the number of weeks worked in a year (52), I needed to calculate Trisha's annual take-home pay. \n\nFirst, I calculated her weekly gross pay: $15/hour * 40 hours/week = $600/week. This was a straightforward multiplication to find her gross earnings before any deductions.\n\nNext, I determined the amount withheld for taxes, unemployment insurance, and social security: 20% of $600 = 0.20 * $600 = $120. This calculation involved converting the percentage to a decimal and then multiplying it by her weekly gross pay to find the total amount withheld.\n\nSubtracting the withheld amount from her weekly gross pay gave me her weekly take-home pay: $600 - $120 = $480. This subtraction provided the net amount she would receive each week after deductions.\n\nTo find her annual take-home pay, I multiplied her weekly take-home pay by the number of weeks in a year: $480/week * 52 weeks/year = $24,960/year. This multiplication gave me the total amount she would receive over the course of a year after all deductions.\n\nTo verify the result, I checked that the weekly gross pay calculation, the percentage calculation, and the final multiplication all used the correct values and operations. The result of $24,960 seemed reasonable given the context of the problem.\n\nThe operations I used were reliable and reusable for similar problems involving gross pay, deductions, and annual calculations. I would reuse these operations whenever I need to calculate take-home pay based on hourly wages, weekly hours, and annual work weeks.\n\nFINAL: 24960",
        "target_sha256": "97c87e1935263925ecc02866873ccb368967751e4ca6f5e9422f494440b99a94"
      }
